132
00:09:45,300 --> 00:09:53,900
In this week's podcast I have a reflection about the passing of Nelson Mandela this past week

133
00:09:53,900 --> 00:09:56,800
And we have some email questions to answer.

134
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:59,400
So thank you very much for listening.

135
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:01,800
And let's get started, shall we?

136
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:12,800
The Scout movement is forming a personal tie between the different foreign countries,

137
00:10:12,800 --> 00:10:18,600
a living force, a great brotherhood of service. a joyous work.

138
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:32,800
Now it would be hard for anyone to have missed the news that former president of South Africa,

139
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:37,900
Nelson Mandela, passed away at the age of 95 this week.

140
00:10:37,900 --> 00:10:44,900
I have always looked at the story of his rise from prisoner to president as an exemplification

141
00:10:44,900 --> 00:10:49,400
of the triumph of good ideas and compassion.

142
00:10:49,400 --> 00:10:55,000
Mandela's tenacity and persistence catalyzed not only the transformation of a nation,

143
00:10:55,000 --> 00:10:59,300
but it spread around the world as an inspiration to all of us.

144
00:10:59,300 --> 00:11:05,800
He said this, for to be free is not merely to cast off one's change, but to live in a way

145
00:11:05,800 --> 00:11:09,199
that respects and enhances the freedom of others.

146
00:11:09,199 --> 00:11:14,600
I learned that courage was not the absence of fear, but triumph over it.

147
00:11:14,600 --> 00:11:20,800
The brave man is not he who does not feel afraid, but he who conquers that fear.

148
00:11:20,800 --> 00:11:24,800
If you want to make peace with your enemy, you have to work with your enemy,

149
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:26,800
and then he becomes your partner.

150
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:31,100
I always remember the axiom: a leader is like a shepherd:

151
00:11:31,100 --> 00:11:34,900
He stays behind the flock, letting the most nimble go out ahead,

152
00:11:34,900 --> 00:11:41,500
whereupon the others follow, not realizing that all along they were directed from behind.

153
00:11:41,500 --> 00:11:44,200
Nelson Mandela, to my knowledge, was never a scout,

154
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:48,500
but upon becoming president of South Africa in 1994,,

155
00:11:48,500 --> 00:11:55,500
Dr Mandela recognized the pioneering role of scouting in integrating South African society

156
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:59,900
and he accepted the role as the association's patron.

157
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:04,800
And under that patronage the scout movement made tremendous progress

158
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:08,300
as one of the largest youth organizations in South Africa.

159
00:12:08,300 --> 00:12:16,000
You may not know it, but it was the South Africa Scout Association. in 1976.

160
00:12:16,000 --> 00:12:18,000
That's 1976.

161
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:25,500
Long before apartheid ended, they took the courageous and really unprecedented step

162
00:12:25,500 --> 00:12:28,400
of defying that unjust system

163
00:12:28,400 --> 00:12:32,600
and they opened their doors to all young South Africans

164
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:35,900
in a single United Association,

165
00:12:35,900 --> 00:12:40,400
The South African Scouting Association exemplified brave,

166
00:12:40,400 --> 00:12:44,100
exemplified those points of the scout law

167
00:12:44,100 --> 00:12:48,199
that encourage us to treat everyone with dignity

168
00:12:48,199 --> 00:12:51,400
and to support the freedom of all people.

169
00:12:51,400 --> 00:12:55,400
Dr Mandela was awarded with the African Elephant Award,

170
00:12:55,400 --> 00:13:00,900
the highest scouting award given by the Africa Scout Committee on the African Continent.

171
00:13:00,900 --> 00:13:05,900
Scott Tier, who is the Secretary General of the World Organization of the Scouting Movement,

172
00:13:05,900 --> 00:13:07,400
said last week

173
00:13:07,400 --> 00:13:12,699
he is and will remain a great inspiration for all scouts around the world.

174
00:13:12,699 --> 00:13:18,800
Today, over 40 million scouts join the rest of the world in mourning for this great loss

175
00:13:18,800 --> 00:13:22,400
and we pray for his soul to rest in peace.

176
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:27,500
Nelson Mandela's singleness of purpose under impossible odds

177
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:31,700
has long been an inspiration for me and, I know, to many of you.

178
00:13:31,700 --> 00:13:36,400
Born into a system that considered him a second-class citizen and denied him a voice,

179
00:13:36,400 --> 00:13:37,400
he spoke out.

180
00:13:37,400 --> 00:13:42,800
He rose from 27 years of hopelessness and indignity as a prisoner

181
00:13:42,800 --> 00:13:48,200
to the presidency of South Africa on the wings of a simple idea:

182
00:13:48,200 --> 00:13:52,800
Treat everyone fairly and make the world a better place.

183
00:13:52,800 --> 00:13:56,700
If anyone could have been bitter and vengeful,

184
00:13:56,800 --> 00:13:58,800
it would have been Nelson Mandela,

185
00:13:58,800 --> 00:14:07,300
but he exchanged bitterness and vengeance for an unprecedented program of peace and reconciliation.

186
00:14:07,300 --> 00:14:12,000
He's shown us, as scouts and as scouters,

187
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:17,600
that one person's powerful potential can make the world a better place.

188
00:14:17,600 --> 00:14:19,300
He's gone home now,

189
00:14:19,300 --> 00:14:23,400
but the fruits of his labor and his ideal of service

190
00:14:23,400 --> 00:14:28,300
and his commitment to peace and reconciliation will always remain with us.

191
00:14:28,300 --> 00:14:34,900
In 2000,, he was interviewed by Neil Mitchell and here's some of what he said:

192
00:14:34,900 --> 00:14:40,000
Children are the most important asset in any country.

193
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:47,100
Any country that does not care for its children is not worth to be called a country.

194
00:14:47,100 --> 00:14:53,500
Sir, millions of people around the world regard you as their hero.

195
00:14:53,500 --> 00:14:55,800
Who are your heroes?

196
00:14:55,800 --> 00:15:03,900
Well, my heroes are men and women, to whatever station in life they may be,

197
00:15:03,900 --> 00:15:14,100
who are committed to removing some of the most serious socioeconomic issues

198
00:15:14,100 --> 00:15:16,600
that affected the common people.

199
00:15:16,600 --> 00:15:22,000
As long as there are men and women of Calibur

200
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:27,200
who are sensitive to the suffering of the common person,

201
00:15:27,200 --> 00:15:31,700
this world will eventually be what we want it to be.

202
00:15:31,700 --> 00:15:37,600
In all continents, there are capable men and women, experienced,

203
00:15:37,600 --> 00:15:40,700
who are rising to the challenges

204
00:15:40,700 --> 00:15:46,400
which are posed by the numerous problems that affect humanity,

205
00:15:46,400 --> 00:15:54,800
And I'm confident that this world one day will be the type of world we all desire.

206
00:15:54,800 --> 00:15:58,900
So it's up to you and I to be men and women of Calibur

207
00:15:58,900 --> 00:16:06,500
and to invest our time unstintingly with compassion to build the next generation.

208
00:16:06,500 --> 00:16:09,200
That's what we do in scouting

209
00:16:09,200 --> 00:16:11,600
And the efforts that we make as scouters

210
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:18,700
and what we will see the children who are our scouts now realize in their lifetimes

211
00:16:18,700 --> 00:16:22,700
will be well worth every minute we spend

212
00:16:39,200 --> 00:17:02,700
Music.

213
00:17:02,700 --> 00:17:08,700
Write me a letter, send it by name

214
00:17:08,700 --> 00:17:11,000
Email. that is, folks.

215
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:14,200
and here's an answer to one of your emails:

216
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:22,400
Music.

217
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:27,500
Rob Button is with Troop and Pack 46 in Fayetteville, Arkansas,

218
00:17:27,500 --> 00:17:30,800
and he wrote in to say I love the podcast, blog and website

219
00:17:30,800 --> 00:17:33,900
chock full of resources, insight, wit and care.

220
00:17:33,900 --> 00:17:37,600
Can't even begin to count how many times in the last three months

221
00:17:37,699 --> 00:17:43,000
you've blogged or podcasted on the exact issue I was dealing with

222
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,300
or thinking with about my troop.

223
00:17:45,300 --> 00:17:49,899
Let me stop there and observe that, yes, we're all in this together

224
00:17:49,899 --> 00:17:54,600
and these, everything that we encounter, somebody else's encountered before.

225
00:17:54,600 --> 00:17:58,100
So it's not necessarily prescience or magic on my part,

226
00:17:58,100 --> 00:18:02,000
even though I do have agents everywhere.

227
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:05,300
It's just that we are all in this together

228
00:18:05,300 --> 00:18:08,800
and there are always things that we're looking to know

229
00:18:08,800 --> 00:18:14,200
and problems that we're dealing with and challenges that we need to overcome,

230
00:18:14,200 --> 00:18:18,100
and you know that's why I'm here to help us all make that happen.

231
00:18:18,100 --> 00:18:20,899
Rob went on to say: I do have a question:

232
00:18:20,899 --> 00:18:24,700
Have you talked about using the relationship between doing what scouts do

233
00:18:24,700 --> 00:18:26,399
and earning merit badges?

234
00:18:26,399 --> 00:18:30,800
I ask this because we're prepping for a high adventure experience this summer

235
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:33,500
and it seems to make sense for the troop to incorporate

236
00:18:33,500 --> 00:18:37,100
earning backpacking, hiking and cooking merit badges into its planning.

237
00:18:37,100 --> 00:18:38,200
Don't you think,

238
00:18:38,200 --> 00:18:42,200
If the boys are interested in doing this and can plan accordingly,

239
00:18:42,200 --> 00:18:44,800
is there any reason why this is a bad idea?

240
00:18:44,800 --> 00:18:47,300
Again, thanks for all you do and all the best.

241
00:18:47,300 --> 00:18:51,600
Well, thanks, Rob, for being in touch, and that's a great question.

242
00:18:51,600 --> 00:18:56,600
Theoretically, it makes perfect sense to combine all this merit badge work

243
00:18:56,600 --> 00:18:59,200
with the activities that you have planned for the summer.

244
00:18:59,200 --> 00:19:03,700
but you need to be careful of putting the cart before the horse. when you do

245
00:19:03,700 --> 00:19:05,700
High adventure, what does that mean?

246
00:19:05,700 --> 00:19:07,500
That's exciting and engaging.

247
00:19:07,500 --> 00:19:10,000
You're going to go do this great stuff.

248
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,800
You're going to have wonderful challenges to overcome.

249
00:19:12,800 --> 00:19:15,300
You're going to have a great time.

250
00:19:15,300 --> 00:19:17,400
That's the horse, okay.

251
00:19:17,400 --> 00:19:19,200
That's what's motivating the scouts.

252
00:19:19,200 --> 00:19:22,700
They're eager to plan and execute this adventure

253
00:19:22,700 --> 00:19:25,800
Merit badge work and advancement is the cart.

254
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:28,900
because they are participating in a high adventure trip,

255
00:19:28,900 --> 00:19:31,800
because they're doing the things that scouts do,

256
00:19:31,800 --> 00:19:34,900
They are naturally fulfilling requirements towards advancement.

257
00:19:34,900 --> 00:19:39,600
It's just a natural outcome of the activity and that's great.

258
00:19:39,600 --> 00:19:42,700
Now if you announce that you are going to be-

259
00:19:42,700 --> 00:19:47,300
quote- doing merit badges- unquote- as a part of the adventure,

260
00:19:47,300 --> 00:19:50,700
then you're putting the cart before the horse. in my mind,

261
00:19:50,700 --> 00:19:55,800
Just let the natural course of this adventurous activity unfold.

262
00:19:55,800 --> 00:19:58,500
Tell scouts they may find it's a good opportunity

263
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:01,400
to find a counselor and get a merit badge or two

264
00:20:01,400 --> 00:20:05,800
as they go through planning and executing this trip.

265
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:10,000
But don't try to tie anything in the trip to doing requirements.

266
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,600
Let the scouts sort that out for themselves.

267
00:20:12,600 --> 00:20:14,600
Now, when I talk about this,

268
00:20:14,600 --> 00:20:17,800
it seems to be a very fine distinction.

269
00:20:17,800 --> 00:20:19,700
It doesn't seem to be all that important,

270
00:20:19,700 --> 00:20:22,500
but it is incredibly important.

271
00:20:22,500 --> 00:20:25,500
I'll tell you that this is not easy to get.

272
00:20:25,500 --> 00:20:29,400
It takes some thinking and it often takes changing the way

273
00:20:29,400 --> 00:20:31,100
that you approach things.

274
00:20:31,100 --> 00:20:34,000
Remember the cart and the horse.

275
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,400
We want the horse in front of the cart. the excitement,

276
00:20:36,400 --> 00:20:40,300
the engagement and the challenge of these adventures.

277
00:20:40,300 --> 00:20:42,600
The cart is the result of them.

278
00:20:42,600 --> 00:20:44,100
Will they fulfill some requirements?

279
00:20:44,100 --> 00:20:45,700
Well, yeah,

280
00:20:45,700 --> 00:20:48,600
Are they doing it to fill requirements?

281
00:20:48,600 --> 00:20:52,400
No, they're doing it because it's a great thing to do.

282
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:54,300
I hope that helps, Rob.

283
00:20:54,300 --> 00:20:57,100
This is not an easy concept to get.

284
00:20:57,100 --> 00:20:59,600
Once you do, things go sailing right along.

285
00:20:59,600 --> 00:21:02,200
Scouts advance. they get merit badges and everything

286
00:21:02,200 --> 00:21:04,100
just in the same amount that they would

287
00:21:04,100 --> 00:21:06,000
if you started with the merit badge

288
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
and you put that cart before the horse,

289
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:09,899
It really really works.

290
00:21:09,899 --> 00:21:14,399
Rich Billion is with Apple Valley, Minnesota,

291
00:21:14,399 --> 00:21:17,700
Troop 298, and he wrote in to say this.

292
00:21:17,700 --> 00:21:19,899
I've been a scout master for about a year.

293
00:21:19,899 --> 00:21:22,300
I've been reading your blog and listening to podcasts

294
00:21:22,300 --> 00:21:23,500
for about a month.

295
00:21:23,500 --> 00:21:25,000
We have a new committee chair

296
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,100
and both of us feel we need to instill

297
00:21:27,100 --> 00:21:30,600
or install the patrol method more solidly in our troop.

298
00:21:30,600 --> 00:21:32,900
From what I can tell from your material

299
00:21:32,900 --> 00:21:35,500
is that this may be a several year process.

300
00:21:35,500 --> 00:21:37,500
Is there any way to do it faster?

301
00:21:39,100 --> 00:21:39,800
I'll get to that.

302
00:21:39,800 --> 00:21:40,700
I'll get to that.

303
00:21:40,700 --> 00:21:42,400
Another question: Rich says

304
00:21:42,400 --> 00:21:44,700
it seems that boys go through a cycle.

305
00:21:44,700 --> 00:21:46,100
The first years are exciting

306
00:21:46,100 --> 00:21:48,100
and then there's a lull

307
00:21:48,100 --> 00:21:50,500
which tends to happen when they enter high school.

308
00:21:50,500 --> 00:21:53,200
Then they seem to come back into it again.

309
00:21:53,200 --> 00:21:54,900
Any tips on keeping them involved

310
00:21:54,900 --> 00:21:56,600
would be much appreciated.

311
00:21:56,600 --> 00:21:57,800
I think the patrol method

312
00:21:57,800 --> 00:22:00,000
would probably do a lot for this.

313
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,300
Well thanks, Rich for being in touch,

314
00:22:02,300 --> 00:22:05,000
and I got to tell you as a new scout master.

315
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:06,600
you're asking all the right questions.

316
00:22:06,600 --> 00:22:10,500
Implementing the patrol system is a never ending process.

317
00:22:10,500 --> 00:22:11,500
We never arrive,

318
00:22:11,500 --> 00:22:12,600
We just keep moving forward.

319
00:22:12,600 --> 00:22:14,300
There's never really a point

320
00:22:14,300 --> 00:22:15,900
where you stop and say

321
00:22:15,900 --> 00:22:18,900
we are now a youth led patrol system troop,

322
00:22:18,900 --> 00:22:20,400
because there's always another stop

323
00:22:20,400 --> 00:22:22,600
and another challenge waiting for you.

324
00:22:22,600 --> 00:22:26,200
This is usually the single most difficult thing

325
00:22:26,200 --> 00:22:28,300
for adults involved in scouting to figure out.

326
00:22:28,300 --> 00:22:30,600
We tend to look for results,

327
00:22:30,600 --> 00:22:32,800
for something we can quantify or point to

328
00:22:32,800 --> 00:22:34,200
and call progress,

329
00:22:34,200 --> 00:22:36,200
but the target keeps moving.

330
00:22:37,700 --> 00:22:39,700
We also tend to look at things

331
00:22:39,700 --> 00:22:41,100
from an adult perspective

332
00:22:41,100 --> 00:22:44,000
rather than a scout perspective.

333
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:45,600
The scale of challenges

334
00:22:45,600 --> 00:22:47,400
and accomplishments look different

335
00:22:47,400 --> 00:22:49,600
depending on your perspective.

336
00:22:49,600 --> 00:22:51,600
You have to look at what's going on

337
00:22:51,600 --> 00:22:53,000
from a scout's point of view

338
00:22:53,000 --> 00:22:56,700
and that's not easy to do at first.

339
00:22:56,700 --> 00:22:58,100
Rich also said-

340
00:22:58,100 --> 00:22:59,400
funny thing, by the way-

341
00:22:59,400 --> 00:23:01,600
sometimes I learn more from scouts

342
00:23:01,600 --> 00:23:02,800
than they learned from me.

343
00:23:02,800 --> 00:23:04,400
I've been thinking about

344
00:23:04,400 --> 00:23:06,000
the patrol method and all that

345
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:07,300
for a long time.

346
00:23:07,300 --> 00:23:09,300
I was talking to my son

347
00:23:09,300 --> 00:23:11,400
and asking him why he isn't excited

348
00:23:11,400 --> 00:23:13,100
as he has been in the past.

349
00:23:13,100 --> 00:23:15,100
He told me that there tends to be

350
00:23:15,100 --> 00:23:17,200
too much adult participation,

351
00:23:17,200 --> 00:23:18,800
especially on campouts.

352
00:23:18,800 --> 00:23:20,900
Rich, what your son is giving you

353
00:23:20,900 --> 00:23:22,700
is that scout perspective.

354
00:23:22,700 --> 00:23:24,900
He sees things that could be different.

355
00:23:24,900 --> 00:23:26,600
He has a pretty instinctual sense

356
00:23:26,600 --> 00:23:27,400
that there are things

357
00:23:27,400 --> 00:23:29,500
that he and his fellow scouts could

358
00:23:29,500 --> 00:23:30,800
or should be doing,

359
00:23:30,800 --> 00:23:32,200
but they can't quite figure out

360
00:23:32,200 --> 00:23:33,200
what to do next.

361
00:23:33,200 --> 00:23:34,300
You'll also see that

362
00:23:34,300 --> 00:23:35,800
when scouts reach this point,

363
00:23:35,800 --> 00:23:37,400
you found the same thing I have.

364
00:23:37,400 --> 00:23:39,300
It happens when they enter high school

365
00:23:39,300 --> 00:23:40,900
around age 14..

366
00:23:40,900 --> 00:23:42,000
They decide that they've had

367
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:43,500
enough of scouts.

368
00:23:43,500 --> 00:23:44,300
It was fun,

369
00:23:44,300 --> 00:23:46,100
but there's really no challenge left.

370
00:23:46,100 --> 00:23:47,200
What your son

371
00:23:47,200 --> 00:23:48,300
and these older scouts

372
00:23:48,300 --> 00:23:49,800
are looking for

373
00:23:49,800 --> 00:23:52,300
is the chance to do something meaningful

374
00:23:52,300 --> 00:23:53,899
and to take on a challenge,

375
00:23:53,899 --> 00:23:55,000
and leading the troop

376
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:57,100
is the challenge they're looking for

377
00:23:57,100 --> 00:23:58,500
Now. we adults

378
00:23:58,500 --> 00:24:00,700
are obviously better leaders and instructors.

379
00:24:00,700 --> 00:24:01,700
We've been around longer,

380
00:24:01,700 --> 00:24:03,000
We have more experience

381
00:24:03,000 --> 00:24:04,300
and we're smarter.

382
00:24:04,300 --> 00:24:05,600
but when we lead

383
00:24:05,600 --> 00:24:07,500
and instruct the scouts,

384
00:24:07,500 --> 00:24:09,899
they just stand and watch,

385
00:24:09,899 --> 00:24:11,600
and that's what your son sees

386
00:24:11,600 --> 00:24:13,800
too much adult involvement.

387
00:24:13,800 --> 00:24:15,100
What you have to do

388
00:24:15,100 --> 00:24:16,899
is let them lead,

389
00:24:16,899 --> 00:24:18,800
Let the scouts lead.

390
00:24:18,800 --> 00:24:20,899
You have to get out of the way

391
00:24:20,899 --> 00:24:23,899
and keep all the other adults out of the way too.

392
00:24:23,899 --> 00:24:25,200
There's so much to say

393
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:27,399
about what you've asked, Rich,

394
00:24:27,399 --> 00:24:29,200
that I hope you'll keep listening.

395
00:24:29,200 --> 00:24:30,399
I hope you'll keep reading

396
00:24:30,399 --> 00:24:32,100
and keep asking questions.

397
00:24:32,100 --> 00:24:34,300
You're asking all the right ones right now.

398
00:24:34,300 --> 00:24:37,100
The next email question

399
00:24:37,100 --> 00:24:38,700
will be anonymous

400
00:24:38,700 --> 00:24:40,000
and it says:

401
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:41,899
in this month's scouting magazine

402
00:24:41,899 --> 00:24:44,300
they talk about boards of review

403
00:24:44,300 --> 00:24:45,399
and it talks about

404
00:24:45,399 --> 00:24:47,899
who can sit on the board of review.

405
00:24:47,900 --> 00:24:50,500
Now I can't seem to find the statement made

406
00:24:50,500 --> 00:24:51,800
in scouting magazine

407
00:24:51,800 --> 00:24:54,800
anywhere in the Guide to Advancement 2013..

408
00:24:54,800 --> 00:24:56,900
For our boards of review,

409
00:24:56,900 --> 00:24:59,400
we usually have folks who know about scouting

410
00:24:59,400 --> 00:25:01,100
and at least one committee member.

411
00:25:01,100 --> 00:25:02,400
I'm sure there's been a time or two

412
00:25:02,400 --> 00:25:04,900
when no committee members have been available.

413
00:25:04,900 --> 00:25:07,900
We can't see not conducting a board of review

414
00:25:07,900 --> 00:25:09,400
for a scout who's ready

415
00:25:09,400 --> 00:25:10,300
and making them wait

416
00:25:10,300 --> 00:25:12,100
just because we don't have it together

417
00:25:12,100 --> 00:25:13,800
and we don't have the right folks there.

418
00:25:13,800 --> 00:25:17,100
Do I go with what scouting magazine states about this

419
00:25:17,100 --> 00:25:20,000
Or with what the Guide to Advancement says?

420
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:21,900
I can understand your confusion there.

421
00:25:21,900 --> 00:25:24,400
I think, having read the statement

422
00:25:24,400 --> 00:25:26,500
in a scouting magazine

423
00:25:26,500 --> 00:25:29,500
about who sits on a board of review

424
00:25:29,500 --> 00:25:33,100
and reading what the Guide to Advancement says,

425
00:25:33,100 --> 00:25:34,900
they're perfectly consistent,

426
00:25:34,900 --> 00:25:36,700
Because you're not actually doing

427
00:25:36,700 --> 00:25:39,300
what the Guide to Advancement says.

428
00:25:39,300 --> 00:25:40,900
the scouting magazine statement

429
00:25:40,900 --> 00:25:42,400
seems to be a little out of sync

430
00:25:42,400 --> 00:25:44,100
or out of kilter.

431
00:25:44,100 --> 00:25:46,100
So let's talk briefly

432
00:25:46,100 --> 00:25:50,000
about who's supposed to be a member of a board of review.

433
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,600
It needs to have no fewer than three members

434
00:25:52,600 --> 00:25:54,400
and no more than six,

435
00:25:54,400 --> 00:25:57,600
All who have to be at least 21 years of age.

436
00:25:57,600 --> 00:25:58,500
By the way,

437
00:25:58,500 --> 00:26:00,200
this is all coming straight out of

438
00:26:00,200 --> 00:26:02,500
the Guide to Advancement 2013..

439
00:26:02,500 --> 00:26:05,500
Unit leaders and assistants

440
00:26:05,500 --> 00:26:07,300
do not serve on a board of review

441
00:26:07,300 --> 00:26:08,900
for their own unit.

442
00:26:08,900 --> 00:26:10,500
Parents or guardians

443
00:26:10,500 --> 00:26:13,100
don't serve on a board for their son,

444
00:26:13,100 --> 00:26:16,000
The candidate, or his parents or guardians

445
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,300
don't have any part in selecting

446
00:26:18,300 --> 00:26:20,200
board of review members.

447
00:26:20,200 --> 00:26:21,700
So the board's made up of

448
00:26:21,700 --> 00:26:24,100
three to six unit committee members,

449
00:26:24,100 --> 00:26:26,200
and the Guide to Advancement 2013

450
00:26:26,200 --> 00:26:28,300
says no more and no less.

451
00:26:28,300 --> 00:26:30,000
So three to six,

452
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:31,300
And it does say this.

453
00:26:31,300 --> 00:26:32,800
in units with fewer than

454
00:26:32,800 --> 00:26:34,500
three registered committee members

455
00:26:34,500 --> 00:26:36,200
available to serve,

456
00:26:36,200 --> 00:26:37,400
it is permissible

457
00:26:37,400 --> 00:26:40,000
to use knowledgeable parents-

458
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,300
not those of the candidate, of course-

459
00:26:42,300 --> 00:26:43,600
or other adults,

460
00:26:43,600 --> 00:26:44,700
registered or not,

461
00:26:44,700 --> 00:26:47,400
who are at least 21 years of age

462
00:26:47,400 --> 00:26:50,300
and who understand Boy Scouting's aims.

463
00:26:50,300 --> 00:26:52,300
Using unregistered adults

464
00:26:52,300 --> 00:26:53,300
for boards of review

465
00:26:53,300 --> 00:26:55,800
must be the exception, though,

466
00:26:55,800 --> 00:26:57,100
and not the rule.

467
00:26:57,100 --> 00:26:58,800
Registered committee members

468
00:26:58,800 --> 00:27:01,000
familiar with the unit program,

469
00:27:01,000 --> 00:27:02,500
who have had a background check

470
00:27:02,500 --> 00:27:04,500
and who are youth protection trained

471
00:27:04,500 --> 00:27:05,500
are preferred.

472
00:27:05,500 --> 00:27:07,000
Scheduling boards of review,

473
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:08,900
when and where committee members

474
00:27:08,900 --> 00:27:09,700
can attend,

475
00:27:09,700 --> 00:27:11,900
usually alleviates this problem

476
00:27:11,900 --> 00:27:13,400
of not having enough

477
00:27:13,400 --> 00:27:15,200
committee members for the board.

478
00:27:15,200 --> 00:27:17,100
In the case where you don't have

479
00:27:17,100 --> 00:27:20,300
enough registered committee members

480
00:27:20,300 --> 00:27:21,700
to sit on a board of review,

481
00:27:21,700 --> 00:27:23,700
yes, you can substitute some people in,

482
00:27:23,700 --> 00:27:25,300
but that should be.

483
00:27:25,300 --> 00:27:27,400
that should be the exception to the rule

484
00:27:27,400 --> 00:27:28,900
and most of the time,

485
00:27:28,900 --> 00:27:31,100
if we just fix the schedule a little bit

486
00:27:31,100 --> 00:27:33,200
and we plan those boards of review

487
00:27:33,200 --> 00:27:34,600
which I recommend you know

488
00:27:34,600 --> 00:27:35,900
you should have every month,

489
00:27:35,900 --> 00:27:37,200
you should have a date

490
00:27:37,200 --> 00:27:38,200
for those boards of review

491
00:27:38,200 --> 00:27:40,900
and get people signed up to sit on them,

492
00:27:40,900 --> 00:27:43,200
Then then we're making some progress

493
00:27:43,200 --> 00:27:45,300
and you're coming in line

494
00:27:45,300 --> 00:27:48,800
with the policies and procedures of the BSA.

495
00:27:48,800 --> 00:27:50,000
I would love to hear from you

496
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:51,200
with your question

497
00:27:51,200 --> 00:27:53,000
that I can answer here on the podcast.

498
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:54,400
you can get in touch with me.

499
00:27:54,400 --> 00:27:55,700
you'll find out how to do that

500
00:27:55,700 --> 00:27:56,900
in just a moment.

501
00:27:56,900 --> 00:27:58,600
but I want to reemphasize:

502
00:27:58,600 --> 00:28:00,500
especially when I'm talking about

503
00:28:00,500 --> 00:28:02,700
policies and procedures like this,

504
00:28:02,700 --> 00:28:04,800
you need to check anything that I say

505
00:28:04,800 --> 00:28:06,700
against the official resources

506
00:28:06,700 --> 00:28:08,500
that are available to us all.

507
00:28:08,500 --> 00:28:11,600
the guide to advancement 2013

508
00:28:11,600 --> 00:28:13,200
is freely available

509
00:28:13,200 --> 00:28:14,800
as a PDF document.

510
00:28:14,800 --> 00:28:16,800
every single scouter should have it

